// @test: a closure with immutable captures outlives its defining frame
// @description: the escaping half of the #384 record. A closure whose captures are all
//   immutable snapshots carries its own heap environment (refcounted, released when the
//   closure value dies), so returning it and calling it after the defining function has
//   exited is well-defined and leaks nothing. This is the shape the boxed-capture work
//   must keep working; the mutable-capture counterpart is still rejected at compile time
//   (test/lambda/test_closure_mutable_return_rejected.vyb).
// @category: lambda, closure, escape, ownership
// @expect-output: escaped=5
// @expect-return: 0

make()<fn() -> Int> -> {
    c<Int> = 5
    return || -> { return c }
}

main()<Int> -> {
    f<fn() -> Int> = make()
    println("escaped=" + f().to_string())
    return 0
}
